.TH CATMD 1 "October 20, 2026" "catmd 0.1.5" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
catmd \- render Markdown as styled text in the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B catmd
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ FILE " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B catmd
reads Markdown files or standard input and writes a terminal rendering.
It supports CommonMark and GitHub extensions including tables, strikethrough,
task lists, autolinks, footnotes, and alerts.
YAML front matter is hidden; HTML is shown literally.
Links are shown as text plus URL and images as text placeholders.
.PP
Each
.I FILE
is rendered in argument order as an independent document.
Nonempty renderings are separated by a blank line.
With no files, or with a file argument of
.BR \- ,
standard input is read.
Input is decoded as UTF-8, replacing invalid byte sequences.
Unreadable inputs are reported on standard error and skipped; remaining
inputs are still rendered.
Use
.B \-\-
before a filename beginning with a hyphen.
.PP
Paragraphs wrap at word boundaries; long words are not split.
Code blocks are never wrapped.
A fenced code block whose info string names a language
.RB ( rust ,
.BR toml ,
.BR ts ,
.BR swift ,
.BR sh ,
and about two hundred more, matched case-insensitively by grammar name or
file extension) is syntax-highlighted through the
.B syntax_*
theme roles; the language is the first word of the info string, cut at a
comma.
Blocks without a recognized language, indented code blocks, and blocks
marked
.BR text ,
.BR plaintext ,
.BR plain ,
.BR txt ,
or
.B nohighlight
render in the plain
.B code_block
style.
Highlighting never changes the text of a block.
Wide tables may overflow the terminal.
Styling uses foreground colors and text attributes, not background colors.
When styling is disabled, output contains no escape sequences.
.SS Paging
Output is sent through a pager only when standard output is a terminal,
paging is enabled, the terminal height is known, and the rendered output
occupies more rows than that height.
Lines wider than the effective width count as multiple rows.
Piped or redirected output is never paged.
.PP
The pager is selected from
.BR CATMD_PAGER ,
then
.BR PAGER ,
then
.BR less .
Whitespace-only values are ignored.
The command is split into a program and arguments with shell-style quoting,
but no shell is run: pipelines, variable expansion, and redirection are
not interpreted.
A program of
.B cat
disables paging; an invalid command-line quoting syntax also disables paging.
.PP
Colors are resolved against the original terminal before the pager starts.
If the pager cannot be started, a diagnostic is written to standard error
and output is written directly instead.
.B catmd
waits for the pager and does not use the pager's exit status as its own.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-color " WHEN"
Select
.BR auto ,
.BR always ,
or
.BR never .
The default is
.BR auto :
style output only on a terminal whose
.B TERM
is not exactly
.BR dumb ,
subject to the environment overrides below.
.B always
forces styling even into a pipe;
.B never
disables it.
An explicit choice of
.B always
or
.B never
overrides the color environment conventions.
.TP
.BI \-\-color\-depth " DEPTH"
Select
.BR auto ,
.BR 16 ,
.BR 256 ,
or
.BR truecolor .
The default is
.BR auto ,
detected from
.B COLORTERM
and
.BR TERM .
This controls color depth, not whether styling is enabled.
Theme colors are downgraded to the nearest representable color as needed;
ANSI color names remain unchanged.
The built-in theme uses only the 16 ANSI colors.
.TP
.BI \-\-width " N"
Set rendering width in columns to a positive integer.
The default is the standard output terminal's width, then a positive
.BR COLUMNS ,
then 80.
Every effective width below 20 is raised to 20.
Zero and invalid values are usage errors.
.TP
.BI \-\-height " N"
Set the terminal height used for paging decisions to a positive integer.
The default is the standard output terminal's height, then a positive
.BR LINES ,
otherwise unknown (no paging).
This option does not enable paging into pipes.
Zero and invalid values are usage errors.
.TP
.BI \-\-theme " THEME"
Select a theme file path or name; see
.BR FILES
and
.BR THEMES .
A nonempty value takes precedence over
.BR CATMD_THEME ;
otherwise that variable or the built-in theme is used.
Surrounding whitespace is trimmed and empty values are ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-ascii
Use ASCII characters for bullets, quote gutters, rules, and table borders,
regardless of locale.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-pager
Never start a pager.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
A nonempty value disables styling in
.B auto
mode, taking precedence over
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
and
.BR CLICOLOR .
An empty value is ignored.
.TP
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
If
.B NO_COLOR
has not disabled styling, a nonempty value other than
.B 0
forces styling in
.B auto
mode, including into a pipe and when
.BR CLICOLOR=0 .
.TP
.B CLICOLOR
If neither of the preceding overrides applies, the exact value
.B 0
disables styling in
.B auto
mode.
Other values, including empty, leave terminal detection in effect.
.TP
.BR COLORTERM ", " TERM
For automatic color depth, surrounding whitespace is ignored and comparisons
are case-insensitive:
.B COLORTERM=truecolor
or
.BR 24bit ,
or a
.B TERM
containing
.BR direct ,
selects 24-bit RGB.
Otherwise a
.B TERM
containing
.B 256color
selects 256 colors; all other values select 16.
This detection also applies when color is forced into a pipe.
Separately, the exact
.B TERM
value
.B dumb
disables ordinary automatic terminal styling.
.TP
.BR COLUMNS ", " LINES
Fallback width and height when the corresponding option is absent and
standard output's terminal size is unavailable.
Values are trimmed and must be positive integers; invalid or empty values
are ignored.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
The first nonempty value, in that order, determines the default glyph set.
A value containing
.B utf-8
or
.B utf8
(case-insensitive) selects Unicode; other nonempty values select ASCII.
If all are unset or empty, Unicode is used.
.B \-\-ascii
overrides this selection.
.TP
.B CATMD_THEME
Default theme path or name when no nonempty
.B \-\-theme
value is supplied.
Whitespace-only values are ignored.
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " HOME
Base directory for named themes; see
.BR FILES .
Surrounding whitespace is trimmed and empty values are ignored.
.TP
.BR CATMD_PAGER ", " PAGER
Pager command selection; see
.BR Paging .
.B CATMD_PAGER
takes precedence over
.BR PAGER .
.TP
.B LESS
Passed to the pager unchanged when nonempty.
When unset or empty,
.B LESS=FRX
is supplied to preserve colors, quit if output fits one screen, and leave
output visible when quitting
.BR less .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/catmd/themes/NAME.toml
Named theme location when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is nonempty after trimming.
.TP
.I $HOME/.config/catmd/themes/NAME.toml
Fallback named theme location.
If both configuration variables are unavailable or empty, the literal relative
path
.I ~/.config/catmd/themes/NAME.toml
is used; the tilde is not expanded by
.BR catmd .
.PP
A theme value containing a slash or ending in
.B .toml
is a file path, not a theme name.
On Windows a backslash also selects a path.
Other values resolve as
.I NAME.toml
in the themes directory.
For example,
.B dark
is a name and
.B dark.toml
is a path relative to the current directory.
Shell expansion can be used for a home-directory path, for example
.BR "\-\-theme ~/my-theme.toml" .
.SH THEMES
A theme is a TOML document whose top-level keys are rendering roles, plus
an optional
.B [palette]
table of named colors.
Omitted roles retain their built-in styles.
Each supplied role replaces its style entirely:
.B "strong = \(dqred\(dq"
makes strong text red without retaining bold.
.PP
A role value is a bare color or a table with optional
.B fg
and
.B modifiers
keys.
Supported modifiers are
.BR bold ,
.BR dim ,
.BR italic ,
.BR underline ,
and
.BR strikethrough .
A modifiers-only table is valid.
.PP
Colors are one of
.BR black ,
.BR red ,
.BR green ,
.BR yellow ,
.BR blue ,
.BR magenta ,
.BR cyan ,
.BR white ,
their
.B bright_
variants, an integer from 0 to 255, a quoted six-digit
.B #rrggbb
hex value, or a palette name.
Palette names are checked before ANSI names, allowing a palette to redefine
.BR red ,
for example.
Palette entries accept ANSI names, indices, and hex values, but cannot
reference other palette entries.
.PP
Supported roles:
.RS
.nf
heading_marker, heading1, heading2, heading3,
heading4, heading5, heading6, strong, emph,
strikethrough, code, code_block, link_text,
link_url, image, quote_gutter, alert_note,
alert_tip, alert_important, alert_warning,
alert_caution, table_header, table_border, rule,
html, footnote_marker, bullet, list_number,
task_checkbox, syntax_keyword, syntax_string,
syntax_comment, syntax_constant, syntax_type,
syntax_function, syntax_tag
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.B syntax_*
roles style the tokens of highlighted code blocks; tokens no role claims,
and every line of an unhighlighted block, use
.BR code_block .
.PP
Example theme:
.RS
.nf
code = "yellow"
strong = { fg = "red", modifiers = ["bold"] }
heading1 = { fg = "accent", modifiers = ["bold"] }
link_url = { modifiers = ["dim"] }

[palette]
accent = "#00aaff"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Keep
.B [palette]
after role assignments: a TOML table header applies to subsequent keys.
Themes change foreground colors and attributes, not glyphs or backgrounds,
and do not inherit from other files.
Italic and strikethrough rendering depend on terminal support.
.PP
Unreadable files, invalid TOML, unknown roles, keys, modifiers, or colors,
and invalid value shapes produce a diagnostic naming the source and problem,
then exit with status 2 before any standard output.
Selected themes are still loaded and validated when styling is off,
but their styles have no visible effect.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Inputs were read and output delivered successfully.
Help and version requests also succeed.
.TP
.B 1
An input could not be read or an output/pager I/O operation failed.
Unreadable inputs do not prevent processing later inputs.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line usage or an unusable theme.
.PP
A pager startup failure alone does not change the status if direct output
succeeds.
The pager's exit status is ignored.
A closed output pipe is handled quietly and preserves the status determined
by input errors.
.SH EXAMPLES
Render a file or several files in order:
.PP
.RS
.nf
catmd README.md
catmd intro.md chapter-*.md
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read standard input alone or between two files:
.PP
.RS
.nf
printf '# Hello\\n' | catmd
catmd header.md \- footer.md < body.md
.fi
.RE
.PP
Render plain ASCII text at a fixed width without paging:
.PP
.RS
.nf
catmd \-\-color never \-\-ascii \-\-width 80 \-\-no\-pager README.md
.fi
.RE
.PP
Preserve styling through an explicitly invoked pager:
.PP
.RS
.nf
catmd \-\-color always README.md | less \-R
.fi
.RE
.PP
Choose pager arguments for automatic paging:
.PP
.RS
.nf
CATMD_PAGER='less \-S' catmd long.md
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use a theme path or a named default theme:
.PP
.RS
.nf
catmd \-\-theme ./theme.toml \-\-color\-depth 256 README.md
CATMD_THEME=dark catmd README.md
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cat (1),
.BR less (1),
.BR man (1)
